<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="6aa5a47d">&lt;p&gt;
    Development standards document the formal rules that must be followed when developing code or configuring
    packages. These standards may include items such as:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Naming conventions (e.g. files, objects, tables)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Formatting conventions (e.g. indenting)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Required documentation (i.e. comments)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Common shared applications and services
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Application communication and inter-operation
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Optimization / Security
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Access (e.g. single sign-on)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Rules for data values (e.g., organizational elements, master data elements, transaction elements)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Error handling and messaging
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Logging
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Code management (i.e. version control)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="d32af2df"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="8617d99d">&lt;p&gt;
    Depending on the type of development there may already be existing standards based on the language or package being
    implemented that may then be extended as needed by the development organization in order to adopt them to their
    particular environment. In the cases where there are variations or exceptions to these standards those items to be
    specifically called out.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Development standards may contain both general guidelines that serve as a set of best practices and
    principles to guide the development as well as formal rules that must be followed.  In cases where both are
    included it is important to distinguish the two.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Standards should be thorough, endorsed by management, communicated and managed prior to functional specification
    handover to development team.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="ff8974d8"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="ee464f09">&lt;p&gt;
    To increase the overall maintainability and quality of the application objects being developed by providing a
    consistent style of coding. Good development standards will also cover best practices for reducing potential
    system vulnerabilities by taking security and privacy considerations into account.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="dd6bb1e1">&lt;p&gt;
    If a consistent set of standards is not followed there may be:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        issues with the interoperability of code
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        additional effort in code creation by duplicating existing functionality by not using existing components and
        services
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        increased effort in performing code reviewing or maintaining code due to increased inconsistencies and variations
        from standard conventions as well as lack of standard documentation
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        increased potential for errors due to invalid assumptions
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="578e1725">&lt;p&gt;
    This work product is not needed if no coding is taking place.  In many cases existing standards will exist that
    can be adopted so this work product will not have to be developed from scratch.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
